      "math_academy_id": "q-15877",
      "knowledge_point_id": "4d63e99a-9db4-565e-97c5-ffc3090b0074",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Substitution",
      "problem": "${\\int }_{5}^{8}\\frac{-4}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x-4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\text{d}u & =\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x-4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 5 & 8 \\\\ u & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{5}^{8}\\frac{-4}{(x-4)}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{-4}{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{-4}{u}\\,\\text{d}u & =-4{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u){|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
