    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-336745",
      "knowledge_point_id": "4d63e99a-9db4-565e-97c5-ffc3090b0074",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Substitution",
      "problem": "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{6x}{\\sqrt{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\text{d}u & =2x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{2}+1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{6x}{\\sqrt{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}3{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u & ={-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{u}}|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{2}}+\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =6-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =6-3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
